[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Connecting to Databases in PHP (MySQLi, PDO)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"PHP offers several ways to connect to databases. Two popular choices are MySQLi (MySQL Improved) and PDO (PHP Data Objects). MySQLi is specific to MySQL databases, while PDO provides a database-agnostic interface, allowing you to switch between database systems with minimal code changes."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"MySQLi Extension"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"MySQLi offers both procedural and object-oriented interfaces. Here's how to connect using both:"},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Procedural Style"},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"<?php
$servername = "localhost";
$username = "username";
$password = "password";
$database = "database_name";

// Create connection
$conn = mysqli_connect($servername, $username, $password, $database);

// Check connection
if (!$conn) {
 die("Connection failed: " . mysqli_connect_error());
}

echo "Connected successfully";

mysqli_close($conn);
?>"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Replace 'localhost', 'username', 'password', and 'database_name' with your actual database credentials."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The 'mysqli_connect_error()' function returns an error message if the connection fails."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Object-Oriented Style"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"<?php
$servername = "localhost";
$username = "username";
$password = "password";
$database = "database_name";

// Create connection
$conn = new mysqli($servername, $username, $password, $database);

// Check connection
if ($conn->connect_error) {
 die("Connection failed: " . $conn->connect_error);
}

echo "Connected successfully";

$conn->close();
?>"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The object-oriented style uses the 'mysqli' class to create a connection."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The '$conn->connect_error' property returns an error message if the connection fails."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"PDO (PHP Data Objects)"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"PDO provides a consistent interface for accessing different databases. Here's how to connect to a MySQL database using PDO:"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"<?php
$servername = "localhost";
$username = "username";
$password = "password";
$database = "database_name";

try {
 $conn = new PDO("mysql:host=$servername;dbname=$database", $username, $password);
 // set the PDO error mode to exception
 $conn->setAttribute(PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE, PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION);
 echo "Connected successfully";
} catch(PDOException $e) {
 echo "Connection failed: " . $e->getMessage();
}

$conn = null;
?>"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The PDO constructor takes a Data Source Name (DSN) as its first argument. The DSN specifies the database type, host, and database name."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Setting 'PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE' to 'PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION' enables exception handling for database errors."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The 'try...catch' block handles potential exceptions during the connection process."},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Always remember to close your database connections after you are done using them to free up resources. In MySQLi, you use 'mysqli_close($conn)' or '$conn->close()'. In PDO, you set the connection variable to null: '$conn = null;'."},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"When choosing between MySQLi and PDO, consider the following: If you are only working with MySQL, MySQLi might be slightly faster. If you need to support multiple database systems, PDO is the better choice due to its database-agnostic nature."}]
